cluding government including politics  the political party you say have lapsed  into what I've called that crisis of  authority it seems that this is a double  edged sword though in the sense that on  the one hand the Internet has greatly  democratized reporting information  availability diversity of sources so on  and so forth and at the same time this  has been a catalyst for the crisis of  fake news and disinformation the  inability to distinguish fact fiction  opinion and reporting so on and so forth  how what's your sense of the balance of  those two forces well I mean I think  there is no balance I think we are in a  totally fractured environment when you  have a crisis of authority you are bound  to get a crisis of uncertainty okay so  you people live in what I call the  public it I don't mean by that the  people I don't mean by that the masses  the public is essentially the old mass  audience which in my day when I was  younger it was like an enormous mirror  that you saw yourself reflected in well  that mirror has shattered has fallen it  has shattered and the public lives in  the broken pieces and each piece seems  to have its own way of collecting and  analyzing information that can be very  different from all the others the pieces  are mutually hostile  and barely sometimes connect about what  is a fact and what isn't one of the  things that I find really interesting  that you write about is that it is much  easier for large groups of the public to  unify around something that they are  against something that they reject than  it is around sort of positive things  that that they want to do and this you  know when you when you look at for  example local news and you see how  sensationalism and stories of crime and  negativity do far better than other  types of stories  there seems to be something inherent  there to the way that people are  motivated to say to their neighbor hey  did you see this bad thing that's going  on that is much more there's something  about that that Spurs action much more  than hey let's get together and do this  positive thing do you agree with that  assessment and what what are the  implications of that when it comes to  activism well when you look at that  fractured environment I just described  each one of those fragments has its own  you know you could be the Tea Party you  could be a feminist you could be Bernie  bro there is very very different ways of  parsing information and data and if you  want to mobilize that unify this very  fractured environment the only way to do  so is by being against and there is this  tremendous swelling of anger against the  established order so what would you have  in many of these revolts and you can  start with Tahrir Square in Egypt and  all the way to Hong Kong you have people  from every kind of political opinion but  they are unified against they want to  overthrow the system as they exist the  danger as you point out is if you're  against and you are as these people tend  to be not interested in alternatives  then you can run into what I call  nihilism which is basically the belief  that the destruction of the system for  its own sake is a form of progress and I  mean I leave you to consider that I  don't think it is well that seems to be  one of the big divisions that exists on  the Left right now which is the work  with the system to improve it mentality  compared to the burn it all down to  eventually rebuild something better  ideology I don't have any moral  imperative one way or the other I think  if you look at history the incremental  ISM seems to have achieved more as I  understand it but I'm not ideologically  or emotionally attached to one movement  or the other but that that seems to be a  very prominent discussion happening  right now on the left  well you burn it all down then what  like the the the difference between  today and say 1917 you were a Bolshevik  or even 1959 you were a Castro issed  you know is you have a an organization a  program and a set of ideologies and you  want power to put those in place you  tear it all down today what do you get  you you basically have people who are  activists in condemning the government  and then turning around and asking the  same government that they are condemning  well we need you to do this and that and  the other verses I want to take over the  era of revolution in my in my humble  opinion as an analyst pretty much died  in 1991 so you're not going to do the  Bolsheviks or Havana at Cubans 59 over  again  so what the public can't yet do that  maybe it should be working towards is  articulate positive demands what is it  that it can actually share together when  it rises up duck it doesn't hong kong  like it doesn't until a for example well  the one that now seems to be potentially  on the cusp is the movement in the  united states for some kind of  single-payer healthcare system or  medicare for all which is as it sees  itself anyway it sees itself as a  positive movement it wants to do  something good it is a movement that  believes that can improve things for  people if the system is changed but at  the same time it's been born in  opposition to the status quo  so do you would you consider that  movement a movement for something or is  it still a movement against i mean i i  find that kind of a circular thing  you're saying that the people in charge  are corrupt that's usually the charge  has made the they are incompetent they  are distant and self-serving okay and  yet you're saying well but i but i have  this massive government program that I  want to hand to them that that just  doesn't work I mean my feeling is you're  talking policy and that that part of  what depresses me a lot about the  political environment this that's the  standard way of talking you talk policy  I'm I'm you know for Medicare I'm  against Medicare I wanna up and I want  to lower it that's fine but what you  have today is a tremendous rebellion  against the distance 